much of what we know about durian comes  from those Hurricane Hunters who track  storms from the sky Errol Barnett was  aboard a US Air Force plane that flew  right into the eye of the hurricane he's  now at Keesler Air Force Base in Biloxi  Mississippi  Errol better you than me it sounds like  a dangerous mission good morning well  Toni it sounds dangerous because it is  the aircraft that went into Dorian  before us had to return to base because  of a mechanical issue but let me show  you what we're dealing with here that  military folks will recognize these  aircraft as Hercules c-130s but they've  been modified they're wc-130 js  so that they can take on hurricanes one  of the modifications this external fuel  tank it's one of two and it allows the  aircraft to stay in the air for up to 14  hours making for one truly epic flight  as the 53rd weather reconnaissance  squadron begins their ascent the  menacing target of their mission  hurricane Dorian threatens 25 million  people in its projected path about  10,000 feet in the sky chief pilot  Lieutenant Colonel Jeff Ragusa and his  crew are collecting data to track the  storms impact around the clock helping  people in Florida and Georgia  information that they need to know the  crew drops GPS sensors like these while  flying directly through the Hurricanes I  to its edge and back again as many as  four times they gather information about  the storms speed direction and winds if  you take a look at this radar behind me  you can see that we are in the eye wall  of Hurricane Dorian and you can feel the  turbulence increase the light is  diminishing and it really gives you a  sense for just how unstable the middle  of these massive storms can be up here  the scenery can change within moments  from the gray and choppy eye wall to the  storms eerily bright and serene eye on  the ground violent and dangerous winds  are ravaging the Bahamas  Dorian's next target the United States  and as we left the Bahamas we caught  sight of this sunset over Florida a  reminder that even nature's beauty can  portray approaching danger and one of  those dangers includes thunderstorms and  lightning take a look at this video shot  onboard one of these hurricane hunter  planes on Sunday the cockpit lit up with  this bright purple light because of  thunderstorms taking place around it  these are the only aircraft in the world  permitted to fly through storms like  this there are 12 of them most other  aircraft have to stay 10 to 20 miles  away a Maria Elena this is the next  aircraft to head toward hurricane durian  it takes off here at noon local time  amazing Errol thank you very much
 

